1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:01,680
Léa: Hello everyone!

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:03,900
Welcome to The Cozy French,

3
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,530
your little get-together

4
00:00:05,530 --> 00:00:07,920
to learn French through

5
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:11,240
natural, warm conversations.

6
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:13,310
I'm Léa, and I'm very happy

7
00:00:13,310 --> 00:00:15,400
to be with you again today.

8
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:16,680
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu!

9
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:18,110
Hello, dear friends!

10
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:19,820
Whether you're at home,

11
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:20,960
on public transport

12
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:23,640
or making your coffee,

13
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:25,480
make yourselves comfortable.

14
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:29,380
We're delighted to spend this time with you.

15
00:00:29,430 --> 00:00:31,960
Léa: Mathieu, today I'd like to start

16
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:33,080
with a question.

17
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:34,530
When you were a child,

18
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:36,910
what job did you want to do later?

19
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:37,200
Mathieu: Ah!

20
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:40,670
You really want to know my childhood dreams?

21
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:43,710
Léa: Of course! Why the hesitation?

22
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:48,200
Mathieu: Because I changed my mind so many times

23
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,460
that my family didn't know what to think.

24
00:00:51,510 --> 00:00:55,060
At six, I wanted to be a firefighter.

25
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:56,990
At eight, an explorer.

26
00:00:56,990 --> 00:00:59,800
And at ten, a chef.

27
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:02,280
Léa: A chef? That doesn't surprise me,

28
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,680
given your love of food!

29
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:06,600
Mathieu: Exactly!

30
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,930
But I thought a chef mostly spent

31
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:13,120
his time tasting delicious dishes.

32
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:14,920
Léa: You forgot that you also

33
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:16,420
had to cook them!

34
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:18,600
Mathieu: Yes. When my mum explained

35
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:21,960
that you had to work for hours,

36
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:25,520
clean and organise a kitchen,

37
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:28,920
my enthusiasm dropped a little.

38
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:31,910
Léa: And today, do you still like cooking?

39
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:34,760
Mathieu: Yes, but calmly at home,

40
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:38,280
without twenty customers waiting for dinner.

41
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:40,950
What about you, Léa? What was your dream?

42
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:42,290
Léa: For a long time,

43
00:01:42,290 --> 00:01:44,540
I wanted to be a vet.

44
00:01:44,590 --> 00:01:46,960
I loved animals and I dreamed

45
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:49,920
of caring for every cat in the neighbourhood.

46
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:51,670
Mathieu: Every cat?

47
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:54,480
Your parents must have been thrilled!

48
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:55,620
Léa: Not really!

49
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:57,820
But as I grew up, I understood

50
00:01:57,820 --> 00:01:59,170
that this job required

51
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:01,600
a lot of scientific knowledge

52
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:03,260
and that sometimes you had

53
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:05,360
to go through difficult situations.

54
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,150
Mathieu: So you changed your mind.

55
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:10,560
Léa: Yes. But I kept something from that dream:

56
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:14,020
the wish to help and understand others.

57
00:02:14,070 --> 00:02:17,160
Mathieu: That's interesting. The job changed,

58
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:20,440
but part of your dream stayed.

59
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:21,370
Léa: Exactly.

60
00:02:21,370 --> 00:02:24,280
And that's precisely our topic today:

61
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:25,950
"What's Your Dream Job?",

62
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,160
in other words,

63
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:29,470
"What is the job of your dreams?"

64
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:32,860
Mathieu: A very simple question when you're a child,

65
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:34,610
but much more complicated

66
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:36,320
when you become an adult.

67
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:38,480
Léa: Because you have to think about our passions,

68
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:42,100
our skills, our financial situation

69
00:02:42,150 --> 00:02:45,200
and sometimes our family's expectations.

70
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:48,320
Mathieu: Not to mention an important question:

71
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:49,510
would we really enjoy this job

72
00:02:49,510 --> 00:02:51,990
every single day?

73
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,980
Léa: Today, we're going to talk about all that,

74
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:56,120
with stories

75
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:58,640
and useful French expressions

76
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:01,320
to talk about work and the future.

77
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:03,410
Mathieu: And if you're learning French,

78
00:03:03,410 --> 00:03:05,200
just listen calmly.

79
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:08,140
No need to understand every word.

80
00:03:08,190 --> 00:03:12,040
Just try to follow our conversation.

81
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:13,830
Léa: Before we start, tell us

82
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:15,280
in the comments:

83
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:17,090
what job did you dream of doing

84
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:18,630
when you were a child?

85
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:22,630
Mathieu: And has your dream changed since then?

86
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,630
Now, let's begin!

87
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:25,690
Léa: So, Mathieu,

88
00:03:25,690 --> 00:03:29,030
why did you want to be an explorer?

89
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:32,500
Mathieu: I think I mostly loved adventure.

90
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:35,580
I wanted to discover new places,

91
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:37,380
meet people

92
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:40,200
and see extraordinary things.

93
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:42,370
Léa: So what you really liked

94
00:03:42,370 --> 00:03:44,080
was discovery.

95
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:45,690
Mathieu: Exactly.

96
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:49,080
I hadn't thought about the job's difficulties.

97
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:51,550
Léa: And why did you want to be a firefighter?

98
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:54,940
Mathieu: Because I admired brave people

99
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:57,280
who come to help others.

100
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:59,580
Léa: There you go. Behind your dreams,

101
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:01,460
there were certain values:

102
00:04:01,510 --> 00:04:06,080
curiosity, adventure and the wish to be useful.

103
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:08,270
Mathieu: You know, I'd never looked at

104
00:04:08,270 --> 00:04:10,800
my childhood dreams that way.

105
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:13,320
Léa: And yet they can help us understand

106
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:15,800
what still interests us today.

107
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:19,400
Mathieu: For example, someone who wanted to be a pilot

108
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:21,240
because they loved travelling

109
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:24,200
could end up working in tourism.

110
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:26,590
Léa: Exactly. Or someone

111
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:28,700
who dreamed of being a teacher

112
00:04:28,750 --> 00:04:31,120
might discover that they mostly love

113
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:32,440
explaining things

114
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:35,440
and choose another job in training.

115
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:37,950
Mathieu: So our dreams can evolve

116
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:40,920
without disappearing completely.

117
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:43,360
Léa: Yes. But we also have to accept

118
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:45,840
that a passion won't necessarily become

119
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:47,320
our profession.

120
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:49,150
Mathieu: Ah, that's important.

121
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:51,060
I love taking photos

122
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:52,720
during my walks,

123
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:55,030
but I'm not sure I'd like having to

124
00:04:55,030 --> 00:04:58,060
photograph events every weekend.

125
00:04:58,110 --> 00:05:01,560
Léa: Exactly. Loving an activity freely

126
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:03,490
isn't always the same thing

127
00:05:03,490 --> 00:05:06,320
as having to do it to earn a living.

128
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:08,760
Mathieu: You know, I have a friend who loved drawing

129
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:10,720
when she was a teenager.

130
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:13,200
She wanted to be an illustrator.

131
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:14,350
Léa: And today?

132
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,220
Mathieu: She works in teaching,

133
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:18,600
but she still draws in the evening.

134
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:21,400
She even makes little cards for her friends.

135
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:23,030
Léa: And is she happy like that?

136
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:26,460
Mathieu: Yes. She says drawing is still a pleasure

137
00:05:26,510 --> 00:05:29,120
because she can do it without pressure.

138
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:31,520
Léa: That's a lovely way to see things.

139
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:33,310
Our work doesn't need

140
00:05:33,310 --> 00:05:35,880
to represent all our passions.

141
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:38,880
Mathieu: Exactly. And I think that removes

142
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:40,560
some of the pressure

143
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:42,800
when you think about your future.

144
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,240
Léa: But there's another factor

145
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:48,640
that strongly influences our career choices.

146
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:51,310
Mathieu: I think I know: family?

147
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:51,820
Léa: Yes.

148
00:05:51,820 --> 00:05:55,580
The expectations of our parents and loved ones.

149
00:05:55,630 --> 00:05:57,460
Mathieu: Ah, the famous discussions

150
00:05:57,460 --> 00:05:59,470
during family meals!

151
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:00,840
When I was a teenager,

152
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:03,240
everyone had an idea for my future.

153
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:05,120
Léa: Really? Tell me!

154
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:06,290
Mathieu: My uncle wanted

155
00:06:06,290 --> 00:06:08,280
me to work in finance.

156
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:09,450
My aunt thought

157
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:12,360
computing would be perfect for me.

158
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:14,050
And my grandfather kept saying

159
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:16,800
you had to choose a stable job.

160
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:18,590
Léa: They probably wanted

161
00:06:18,590 --> 00:06:20,560
you to have a secure future.

162
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:22,560
Mathieu: Yes, I know their advice

163
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:24,340
was well-meant.

164
00:06:24,390 --> 00:06:26,640
But sometimes I felt

165
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:27,880
that everyone knew

166
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:30,520
what I should do, except me.

167
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:31,720
Léa: It's a situation

168
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,080
that many young people know.

169
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,560
They try to understand what they want

170
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:39,360
while listening to their family's advice.

171
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:41,180
Mathieu: And it's not always easy

172
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:42,560
to tell the difference

173
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:44,520
between what we really want

174
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:47,040
and what others expect from us.

175
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:50,390
Léa: Exactly. Parents often think

176
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:52,350
about stability, salary

177
00:06:52,350 --> 00:06:54,840
and job opportunities.

178
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:56,880
Those are important questions.

179
00:06:56,880 --> 00:07:00,040
Mathieu: But we also have to think about our personality

180
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:01,400
and what we enjoy.

181
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:04,470
Léa: Yes. Because we're the ones who'll do

182
00:07:04,470 --> 00:07:06,440
this job every day.

183
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:08,100
Mathieu: And a prestigious job

184
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:10,720
won't necessarily make us happy.

185
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:11,860
Léa: Very true.

186
00:07:11,910 --> 00:07:13,500
"Prestigious" means

187
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:15,470
that a job or a position

188
00:07:15,470 --> 00:07:19,080
is especially respected by society.

189
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:22,520
Mathieu: For example, a job can be prestigious

190
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:25,280
but not match our needs.

191
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:27,690
Léa: Exactly. I know someone

192
00:07:27,690 --> 00:07:30,400
who studied law for several years

193
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:31,660
because his family

194
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:33,920
thought it was the ideal career.

195
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:35,830
Mathieu: And did he become a lawyer?

196
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:37,720
Léa: Yes, for a while.

197
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:40,020
But he discovered he didn't like

198
00:07:40,020 --> 00:07:41,580
the pace of work.

199
00:07:41,630 --> 00:07:44,510
He was more interested in training

200
00:07:44,510 --> 00:07:46,880
and supporting people.

201
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:48,790
Mathieu: So he changed direction?

202
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:50,920
Léa: Yes. Today he works

203
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:53,320
in professional training.

204
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:55,400
And his legal knowledge

205
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:57,120
is still useful to him.

206
00:07:57,120 --> 00:07:59,360
Mathieu: So changing jobs

207
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:01,560
doesn't mean all our previous studies

208
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:03,510
were useless.

209
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:04,790
Léa: Absolutely.

210
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:07,150
We can use our skills

211
00:08:07,150 --> 00:08:09,180
in different fields.

212
00:08:09,230 --> 00:08:12,520
Mathieu: But I think we also have to be realistic.

213
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:14,010
We can't always change

214
00:08:14,010 --> 00:08:15,600
careers right away.

215
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:17,980
Léa: That's true. Some people have

216
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:20,040
family responsibilities,

217
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:22,160
financial obligations

218
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:25,000
or need extra training.

219
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,660
Mathieu: So sometimes you have to

220
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:29,320
prepare your change gradually.

221
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:30,510
Léa: Exactly.

222
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:33,000
Listening to what you want doesn't mean

223
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,460
ignoring the realities of life.

224
00:08:35,510 --> 00:08:36,830
Mathieu: Which brings us

225
00:08:36,830 --> 00:08:40,440
to a very important topic: money!

226
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:42,640
Léa: I knew you were going to say that!

227
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:43,920
Mathieu: But it's true!

228
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:46,360
You can be passionate about a job,

229
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:48,960
but you also have to pay for your home,

230
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:50,680
buy food

231
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:52,720
and take care of your family.

232
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:53,790
Léa: Absolutely.

233
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:56,920
Financial stability is important,

234
00:08:56,920 --> 00:08:59,840
and our career choices also depend

235
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:01,580
on our situation.

236
00:09:01,630 --> 00:09:02,820
Mathieu: By the way, Léa,

237
00:09:02,820 --> 00:09:06,070
if money weren't a problem at all,

238
00:09:06,070 --> 00:09:07,870
what job would you like to try?

239
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:11,420
Léa: Hmm... I think I'd like to work

240
00:09:11,420 --> 00:09:13,600
in a small publishing house.

241
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,390
Mathieu: Ah, that suits you well.

242
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:17,110
You love books.

243
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:20,520
Léa: Yes. I'd like to discover new authors,

244
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:22,440
talk about their stories

245
00:09:22,440 --> 00:09:25,760
and help create beautiful books.

246
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:27,490
Mathieu: I can picture you perfectly

247
00:09:27,490 --> 00:09:29,540
in an office full of books,

248
00:09:29,590 --> 00:09:32,710
with a big window and a cup of tea.

249
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:35,710
Léa: Exactly! And you?

250
00:09:35,760 --> 00:09:38,620
Mathieu: Me, I'd like to create a show

251
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:40,450
about travel

252
00:09:40,450 --> 00:09:42,880
and culinary traditions.

253
00:09:42,880 --> 00:09:45,350
Léa: Food again!

254
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:47,510
Mathieu: But this time, I'm serious!

255
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:49,840
I'd like to meet people,

256
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:52,410
listen to their stories and discover

257
00:09:52,410 --> 00:09:55,940
how they cook traditional dishes.

258
00:09:55,990 --> 00:09:57,800
Léa: In fact, this project brings together

259
00:09:57,800 --> 00:09:59,520
several of your passions:

260
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:01,180
travelling, communicating

261
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:03,920
and discovering new cultures.

262
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:07,080
Mathieu: Exactly. But in reality,

263
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:09,770
this kind of work takes time,

264
00:10:09,770 --> 00:10:13,320
resources and a lot of organisation.

265
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:15,880
Léa: That's why you have to find a balance

266
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:18,960
between your wishes and your material needs.

267
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:20,790
Mathieu: And sometimes, a job

268
00:10:20,790 --> 00:10:22,800
you don't absolutely love

269
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:25,620
can give us precious things.

270
00:10:25,670 --> 00:10:27,090
Léa: Like a stable salary,

271
00:10:27,090 --> 00:10:28,720
reasonable hours

272
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:31,040
or more time with your family.

273
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:33,260
Mathieu: Not to mention a short commute

274
00:10:33,260 --> 00:10:35,840
between home and work.

275
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:38,150
Two hours of travel a day

276
00:10:38,150 --> 00:10:39,840
can be exhausting!

277
00:10:39,840 --> 00:10:41,550
Léa: That's true. The lifestyle

278
00:10:41,550 --> 00:10:42,840
that comes with a job

279
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:45,720
is as important as the job itself.

280
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:48,160
Mathieu: You know, I know two people

281
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:50,760
who have completely different ideas

282
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:52,960
of professional success.

283
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:53,860
Léa: Oh really?

284
00:10:53,910 --> 00:10:56,640
Mathieu: The first one loves her job.

285
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:58,700
She manages big projects,

286
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:02,600
travels often and loves responsibility.

287
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:03,750
Léa: And the second one?

288
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:05,600
Mathieu: She prefers a job

289
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:08,040
with regular hours.

290
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:10,730
She finishes early enough to spend time

291
00:11:10,730 --> 00:11:13,760
with her children and enjoy her hobbies.

292
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:15,990
Léa: And are they both satisfied?

293
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:16,490
Mathieu: Yes.

294
00:11:16,490 --> 00:11:20,580
They simply have different priorities.

295
00:11:20,630 --> 00:11:22,280
Léa: There you go. There isn't

296
00:11:22,280 --> 00:11:25,280
just one definition of success.

297
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:28,690
Mathieu: So an ideal job isn't necessarily

298
00:11:28,690 --> 00:11:32,000
the one that impresses others the most.

299
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,840
Léa: Exactly. It's the one

300
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:36,620
that fits our needs well enough,

301
00:11:36,620 --> 00:11:39,240
our values and our way of life.

302
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:40,410
Mathieu: But sometimes,

303
00:11:40,410 --> 00:11:43,680
a job looks wonderful from the outside

304
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:46,780
while the reality is very different.

305
00:11:46,830 --> 00:11:48,420
Léa: Oh yes. We often see

306
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:51,440
the most pleasant sides of a profession.

307
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:54,520
Mathieu: For example, working in tourism.

308
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:57,040
We imagine gorgeous hotels,

309
00:11:57,040 --> 00:11:59,560
travel and meeting people.

310
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:02,550
Léa: But we sometimes forget the irregular hours,

311
00:12:02,550 --> 00:12:03,860
difficult customers

312
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:05,840
or tiring trips.

313
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:09,720
Mathieu: Exactly. Or being a chef!

314
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:11,390
We see the beautiful dishes,

315
00:12:11,390 --> 00:12:13,610
but rarely the long hours

316
00:12:13,610 --> 00:12:15,620
standing in a kitchen.

317
00:12:15,670 --> 00:12:17,520
Léa: That's why it's important

318
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:19,750
to discover the reality of a job

319
00:12:19,750 --> 00:12:22,280
before making a big decision.

320
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:24,540
Mathieu: You know, a friend of mine dreamed

321
00:12:24,540 --> 00:12:27,400
of opening a flower shop.

322
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:28,910
Léa: Oh, how charming!

323
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:31,910
Mathieu: Yes. She imagined spending her days

324
00:12:31,910 --> 00:12:34,450
surrounded by gorgeous flowers,

325
00:12:34,450 --> 00:12:36,800
making bouquets for her customers.

326
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:38,470
Léa: And did she open her shop?

327
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:39,880
Mathieu: Not right away.

328
00:12:39,880 --> 00:12:42,350
She first worked for a few weeks

329
00:12:42,350 --> 00:12:45,260
at a florist's to discover the job.

330
00:12:45,310 --> 00:12:47,160
Léa: That was a very good idea.

331
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:48,840
Mathieu: Yes. And she discovered

332
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:51,160
that you had to get up very early,

333
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:54,080
carry boxes, handle orders

334
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:57,360
and sometimes work very long days.

335
00:12:57,360 --> 00:12:59,220
Léa: So the job was more demanding

336
00:12:59,220 --> 00:13:00,760
than she imagined.

337
00:13:00,760 --> 00:13:04,040
Mathieu: Exactly. She still loved flowers,

338
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:06,430
but she understood she preferred

339
00:13:06,430 --> 00:13:09,360
creating arrangements for events

340
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:12,300
rather than running a shop every day.

341
00:13:12,350 --> 00:13:15,360
Léa: That's an excellent example.

342
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:18,180
Sometimes, trying an activity helps us

343
00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:21,880
better understand what we really want.

344
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:24,020
Mathieu: Yes. And that doesn't mean

345
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:26,570
our dream was ridiculous.

346
00:13:26,570 --> 00:13:28,430
We simply learned

347
00:13:28,430 --> 00:13:30,200
something new.

348
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:31,880
Léa: Exactly.

349
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:33,780
That's why it can be useful

350
00:13:33,780 --> 00:13:36,040
to talk with professionals,

351
00:13:36,040 --> 00:13:38,200
take a short course

352
00:13:38,200 --> 00:13:39,860
or do an internship.

353
00:13:39,910 --> 00:13:43,480
Mathieu: Because a job isn't just its image.

354
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:46,220
Léa: Exactly. You also have to imagine

355
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:49,160
a normal day in that profession.

356
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:50,960
Mathieu: Not just the first day,

357
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:53,360
when everything is new and exciting.

358
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:57,040
Léa: Exactly. Imagine an ordinary Tuesday,

359
00:13:57,040 --> 00:13:59,440
after six months on the job.

360
00:13:59,440 --> 00:14:01,050
Do the daily tasks

361
00:14:01,050 --> 00:14:02,380
still interest you?

362
00:14:02,430 --> 00:14:05,310
Mathieu: Ah, that question is very useful.

363
00:14:05,310 --> 00:14:08,300
Because even an exciting job

364
00:14:08,300 --> 00:14:11,320
has ordinary days.

365
00:14:11,320 --> 00:14:14,280
Léa: And sometimes, difficult days.

366
00:14:14,280 --> 00:14:15,930
Mathieu: You know, that brings us

367
00:14:15,930 --> 00:14:17,920
to talk about personality.

368
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:21,260
Léa: Yes. Because a job that's ideal for one person

369
00:14:21,260 --> 00:14:24,120
can be very hard for another.

370
00:14:24,120 --> 00:14:27,110
Mathieu: For example, some people love

371
00:14:27,110 --> 00:14:28,740
speaking in front of an audience.

372
00:14:28,790 --> 00:14:29,680
Léa: Whereas others

373
00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:31,790
prefer to work quietly,

374
00:14:31,790 --> 00:14:33,760
in a calmer environment.

375
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:35,680
Mathieu: Me, I like communicating,

376
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:37,670
but I wouldn't want to spend

377
00:14:37,670 --> 00:14:40,000
my whole day on the phone.

378
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,350
Léa: And I like organising ideas

379
00:14:42,350 --> 00:14:44,260
and explaining things,

380
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:45,530
but I also need

381
00:14:45,530 --> 00:14:47,440
moments of concentration.

382
00:14:47,440 --> 00:14:49,150
Mathieu: So our personality

383
00:14:49,150 --> 00:14:51,260
can give us clues

384
00:14:51,260 --> 00:14:54,020
about the type of work that suits us.

385
00:14:54,070 --> 00:14:54,980
Léa: Exactly.

386
00:14:54,980 --> 00:14:57,620
Some people like routine.

387
00:14:57,620 --> 00:14:59,880
Others prefer variety.

388
00:14:59,880 --> 00:15:02,630
Mathieu: And some like working in a team,

389
00:15:02,630 --> 00:15:05,880
while others are more efficient alone.

390
00:15:05,880 --> 00:15:06,850
Léa: No preference

391
00:15:06,850 --> 00:15:09,560
is automatically better than another.

392
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:11,380
Mathieu: We simply have to learn

393
00:15:11,380 --> 00:15:12,880
to know ourselves better.

394
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:15,250
Léa: Yes. And it's also important

395
00:15:15,250 --> 00:15:17,520
to identify our strengths.

396
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:20,350
Mathieu: "Strengths" are our qualities

397
00:15:20,350 --> 00:15:23,220
and our particular skills.

398
00:15:23,270 --> 00:15:26,880
Léa: Exactly. Some people are organised,

399
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:28,440
others are creative

400
00:15:28,440 --> 00:15:30,760
or especially patient.

401
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:33,480
Mathieu: And sometimes, we don't even notice

402
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:35,240
our own qualities.

403
00:15:35,240 --> 00:15:35,990
Léa: Why?

404
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:39,380
Mathieu: Because some things seem easy to us.

405
00:15:39,380 --> 00:15:41,570
We imagine that everyone

406
00:15:41,570 --> 00:15:43,600
has the same abilities.

407
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:46,350
Léa: That's true. For example, someone

408
00:15:46,350 --> 00:15:49,240
who can explain complex ideas clearly

409
00:15:49,240 --> 00:15:51,940
may think it's an ordinary skill.

410
00:15:51,990 --> 00:15:54,720
Mathieu: Whereas it's a real strength.

411
00:15:54,720 --> 00:15:57,410
Léa: A good way to discover your qualities

412
00:15:57,410 --> 00:15:58,610
is to notice

413
00:15:58,610 --> 00:16:01,360
what others often ask us for.

414
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:04,420
Mathieu: For example, if my friends always ask me

415
00:16:04,420 --> 00:16:06,240
to organise the trips,

416
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:08,170
maybe it's because I'm good

417
00:16:08,170 --> 00:16:10,240
at planning itineraries.

418
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:12,740
Léa: And if your colleagues regularly ask you

419
00:16:12,740 --> 00:16:14,160
to proofread their texts,

420
00:16:14,160 --> 00:16:16,720
maybe you have a gift for writing.

421
00:16:16,720 --> 00:16:18,510
Mathieu: And if everyone asks me

422
00:16:18,510 --> 00:16:20,460
to choose the restaurants?

423
00:16:20,510 --> 00:16:23,600
Léa: Then you probably have a special talent

424
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:25,920
for finding good places to eat!

425
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:28,590
Mathieu: Finally, a recognised skill!

426
00:16:28,640 --> 00:16:30,710
Léa: But be careful, our strengths

427
00:16:30,710 --> 00:16:33,640
don't necessarily have to become our job.

428
00:16:33,640 --> 00:16:36,340
Mathieu: Yes. They can simply help us

429
00:16:36,340 --> 00:16:38,920
better understand what we enjoy.

430
00:16:38,920 --> 00:16:42,130
Léa: Exactly. And sometimes we discover

431
00:16:42,130 --> 00:16:45,460
our talents through unexpected experiences.

432
00:16:45,510 --> 00:16:48,780
Mathieu: You know, my cousin had absolutely no idea

433
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:51,720
what job to choose after her studies.

434
00:16:51,720 --> 00:16:54,270
Léa: Oh really? What fields interested her?

435
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:55,980
Mathieu: She liked languages,

436
00:16:55,980 --> 00:16:58,050
drawing and communication,

437
00:16:58,050 --> 00:17:01,400
but she struggled to choose a direction.

438
00:17:01,400 --> 00:17:03,950
Léa: And how did she find her path?

439
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:08,120
Mathieu: She did an internship at a small company.

440
00:17:08,120 --> 00:17:10,830
She had to prepare presentations

441
00:17:10,830 --> 00:17:13,810
and explain complicated information

442
00:17:13,810 --> 00:17:15,340
in a simple way.

443
00:17:15,390 --> 00:17:16,990
Léa: And did she like it?

444
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:18,570
Mathieu: Enormously.

445
00:17:18,570 --> 00:17:20,730
She realised she loved

446
00:17:20,730 --> 00:17:23,040
turning complex ideas

447
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:26,720
into visual content that's easy to understand.

448
00:17:26,720 --> 00:17:29,590
Léa: Ah, that's an interesting skill.

449
00:17:29,640 --> 00:17:31,850
Mathieu: Today, she works

450
00:17:31,850 --> 00:17:34,480
in visual communication.

451
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:36,680
But when she was a teenager,

452
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:39,840
she didn't even really know this job existed.

453
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:41,990
Léa: That shows you can't always

454
00:17:41,990 --> 00:17:45,020
choose a job before knowing it exists.

455
00:17:45,070 --> 00:17:48,690
Mathieu: Exactly. And sometimes our direction

456
00:17:48,690 --> 00:17:52,760
appears gradually through experience.

457
00:17:52,760 --> 00:17:55,650
Léa: That's why there's no need to panic

458
00:17:55,650 --> 00:17:58,680
when you don't know your ideal job yet.

459
00:17:58,680 --> 00:18:00,700
Mathieu: And yet many young people

460
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:02,830
feel enormous pressure

461
00:18:02,830 --> 00:18:05,360
when choosing their studies.

462
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:07,660
Léa: That's true. They're sometimes asked

463
00:18:07,660 --> 00:18:08,960
to imagine their career

464
00:18:08,960 --> 00:18:10,940
for the next ten years

465
00:18:10,990 --> 00:18:12,880
when they're only just starting

466
00:18:12,880 --> 00:18:15,120
to discover the working world.

467
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:18,280
Mathieu: And not everyone moves at the same pace.

468
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:19,600
Léa: Exactly.

469
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:22,440
Some people find their path very early.

470
00:18:22,440 --> 00:18:25,600
Others need several experiences.

471
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:28,120
Mathieu: And some change careers

472
00:18:28,120 --> 00:18:30,880
after ten or fifteen years of work.

473
00:18:30,880 --> 00:18:31,890
Léa: Absolutely.

474
00:18:31,890 --> 00:18:35,040
Our priorities can change over time.

475
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:38,860
Mathieu: At twenty, you may seek adventure.

476
00:18:38,910 --> 00:18:40,920
Later, you may give

477
00:18:40,920 --> 00:18:43,680
more importance to stability.

478
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:45,680
Léa: Or want a more flexible job

479
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:47,960
to spend time with your family.

480
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:50,220
Mathieu: And it's not necessarily a failure

481
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:52,160
to change direction.

482
00:18:52,160 --> 00:18:54,860
Léa: No. It can simply mean

483
00:18:54,860 --> 00:18:57,640
you've learned more about yourself.

484
00:18:57,640 --> 00:19:00,240
Mathieu: You know, I think there's a problem

485
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:02,990
with the expression "dream job".

486
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:04,140
Léa: Which one?

487
00:19:04,190 --> 00:19:06,920
Mathieu: It sometimes gives the impression

488
00:19:06,920 --> 00:19:09,480
that a perfect profession exists somewhere.

489
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:12,360
Léa: An exciting, well-paid job,

490
00:19:12,360 --> 00:19:14,670
with amazing colleagues

491
00:19:14,670 --> 00:19:15,870
and no stress?

492
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:18,870
Mathieu: Exactly! And of course,

493
00:19:18,870 --> 00:19:22,360
lots of holidays and free coffee!

494
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:25,920
Léa: Mathieu, you're asking for an awful lot!

495
00:19:25,920 --> 00:19:29,720
Mathieu: I know. But it's a dream, after all!

496
00:19:29,720 --> 00:19:30,990
Léa: More seriously,

497
00:19:30,990 --> 00:19:33,900
I don't think a perfect job exists.

498
00:19:33,950 --> 00:19:35,360
Mathieu: Me neither.

499
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:37,200
Even a job you love

500
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:39,360
has its difficulties.

501
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:42,560
Léa: There can be deadlines, responsibilities,

502
00:19:42,560 --> 00:19:44,180
repetitive tasks

503
00:19:44,180 --> 00:19:46,360
or very busy periods.

504
00:19:46,360 --> 00:19:49,510
Mathieu: So an ideal job may not be

505
00:19:49,510 --> 00:19:51,760
a job without problems.

506
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:53,080
Léa: Exactly.

507
00:19:53,080 --> 00:19:54,920
It's perhaps rather a job

508
00:19:54,920 --> 00:19:57,670
whose positive sides match

509
00:19:57,670 --> 00:19:59,200
what matters to us,

510
00:19:59,200 --> 00:20:02,180
and whose difficulties stay acceptable.

511
00:20:02,230 --> 00:20:04,160
Mathieu: I like that definition.

512
00:20:04,160 --> 00:20:06,240
It's much more realistic.

513
00:20:06,240 --> 00:20:08,400
Léa: And it reminds us we don't need

514
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:10,500
to find a perfect profession

515
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:12,920
to build a satisfying life.

516
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:16,600
Mathieu: You know, some people like their job,

517
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:18,920
but mostly find happiness

518
00:20:18,920 --> 00:20:19,990
in their hobbies

519
00:20:19,990 --> 00:20:22,320
and their personal relationships.

520
00:20:22,320 --> 00:20:24,480
Léa: And that's perfectly legitimate.

521
00:20:24,480 --> 00:20:26,920
Mathieu: So our job doesn't need

522
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:30,060
to represent our whole identity.

523
00:20:30,110 --> 00:20:31,030
Léa: Absolutely.

524
00:20:31,030 --> 00:20:34,200
But if someone wants to change careers,

525
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:36,280
they can start by thinking about

526
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:39,240
what's missing in their current job.

527
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:42,440
Mathieu: For example, more creativity,

528
00:20:42,440 --> 00:20:45,240
freedom or stability.

529
00:20:45,240 --> 00:20:48,920
Léa: Yes. Then they can look for information,

530
00:20:48,920 --> 00:20:51,000
talk with professionals

531
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,680
and discover the skills needed.

532
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:56,700
Mathieu: And prepare their project gradually,

533
00:20:56,700 --> 00:20:59,820
especially if they have financial responsibilities.

534
00:20:59,870 --> 00:21:01,460
Léa: Exactly.

535
00:21:01,460 --> 00:21:03,270
A career change

536
00:21:03,270 --> 00:21:05,440
doesn't have to be immediate.

537
00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:07,630
Mathieu: And even if you finally decide

538
00:21:07,630 --> 00:21:10,120
to stay in your current job,

539
00:21:10,120 --> 00:21:12,390
this reflection can help us

540
00:21:12,390 --> 00:21:14,880
better understand our needs.

541
00:21:14,880 --> 00:21:16,550
Léa: Absolutely.

542
00:21:16,550 --> 00:21:17,920
Now, I'd like to talk about

543
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:20,410
a few very useful expressions

544
00:21:20,410 --> 00:21:22,040
for our listeners.

545
00:21:22,040 --> 00:21:23,710
Mathieu: Great idea!

546
00:21:23,710 --> 00:21:25,860
Because talking about your work

547
00:21:25,860 --> 00:21:28,100
and your career plans

548
00:21:28,150 --> 00:21:29,600
is a common topic

549
00:21:29,600 --> 00:21:32,200
in French conversations.

550
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:33,990
Léa: For example, to ask

551
00:21:33,990 --> 00:21:37,060
someone's job, you can say:

552
00:21:37,110 --> 00:21:38,990
"What do you do for a living?"

553
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:40,990
Mathieu: Or: "What field

554
00:21:40,990 --> 00:21:42,590
do you work in?"

555
00:21:42,640 --> 00:21:46,100
Léa: And to talk about your ambitions, you can say:

556
00:21:46,150 --> 00:21:48,760
"I'd like to work in tourism."

557
00:21:48,760 --> 00:21:53,660
Mathieu: Or: "My dream would be to become an architect."

558
00:21:53,710 --> 00:21:54,810
Léa: Exactly.

559
00:21:54,810 --> 00:21:57,800
And if you're not sure of your choice yet,

560
00:21:57,800 --> 00:21:58,820
you can say:

561
00:21:58,870 --> 00:22:00,220
"I'm still thinking about

562
00:22:00,220 --> 00:22:02,910
my career path."

563
00:22:02,960 --> 00:22:05,080
Mathieu: "Career guidance"

564
00:22:05,080 --> 00:22:07,040
is looking for a job

565
00:22:07,040 --> 00:22:09,800
or a field that suits us.

566
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:12,330
Léa: Very good. Now, let's review

567
00:22:12,330 --> 00:22:14,600
some natural expressions.

568
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:17,580
Mathieu: Let's start with "trouver sa voie".

569
00:22:17,630 --> 00:22:19,950
Léa: "Trouver sa voie" means discovering

570
00:22:19,950 --> 00:22:23,090
a professional or personal direction

571
00:22:23,090 --> 00:22:24,520
that suits us.

572
00:22:24,520 --> 00:22:28,000
For example: "After several experiences,

573
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,960
she found her path in design."

574
00:22:30,960 --> 00:22:34,310
Mathieu: Second expression: "changer de voie".

575
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:37,480
Léa: It means choosing a new direction,

576
00:22:37,480 --> 00:22:40,400
often in your studies or career.

577
00:22:40,400 --> 00:22:41,490
Mathieu: For example:

578
00:22:41,540 --> 00:22:44,400
"After a few years in sales,

579
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:46,780
he decided to change direction."

580
00:22:46,830 --> 00:22:48,500
Léa: Third expression:

581
00:22:48,550 --> 00:22:50,750
"to flourish in your work".

582
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:53,500
Mathieu: It means feeling good,

583
00:22:53,500 --> 00:22:56,640
growing and finding satisfaction

584
00:22:56,640 --> 00:22:59,360
in your professional activity.

585
00:22:59,360 --> 00:23:02,350
Léa: For example: "She flourishes in her job

586
00:23:02,350 --> 00:23:04,880
because she likes helping others."

587
00:23:04,880 --> 00:23:06,960
Mathieu: Fourth expression:

588
00:23:07,010 --> 00:23:09,870
"to have a sense of something".

589
00:23:09,920 --> 00:23:11,740
Léa: It describes an ability

590
00:23:11,740 --> 00:23:14,140
or a particular quality.

591
00:23:14,190 --> 00:23:17,910
For example: "He has a knack for organising."

592
00:23:17,960 --> 00:23:21,350
Mathieu: Or: "She's good with people."

593
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:24,750
Léa: Fifth expression: "gagner sa vie".

594
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:28,320
Mathieu: It means earning the money you need

595
00:23:28,320 --> 00:23:31,080
to live through your work.

596
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:33,800
Léa: For example: "He'd like to earn a living

597
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:36,150
doing photography."

598
00:23:36,200 --> 00:23:38,850
Mathieu: And our last expression:

599
00:23:38,900 --> 00:23:40,510
"se reconvertir".

600
00:23:40,560 --> 00:23:42,390
Léa: "Se reconvertir" means

601
00:23:42,390 --> 00:23:45,760
changing job or professional field

602
00:23:45,760 --> 00:23:49,360
after already working in another sector.

603
00:23:49,360 --> 00:23:50,450
Mathieu: For example:

604
00:23:50,500 --> 00:23:53,620
"After ten years in finance, she decided

605
00:23:53,620 --> 00:23:56,470
to retrain as a teacher."

606
00:23:56,520 --> 00:23:59,050
Léa: There you go, six very useful expressions

607
00:23:59,050 --> 00:24:02,080
to talk about work and the future.

608
00:24:02,080 --> 00:24:05,030
Mathieu: And now, let's see how to make

609
00:24:05,030 --> 00:24:08,580
some sentences sound more natural in French.

610
00:24:08,630 --> 00:24:11,750
Léa: Let's start with: "I want to be a doctor."

611
00:24:11,800 --> 00:24:15,270
Mathieu: You can say: "I'd like to become a doctor."

612
00:24:15,320 --> 00:24:19,230
Léa: Or: "My dream would be to practise medicine."

613
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:20,760
Mathieu: Second sentence:

614
00:24:20,810 --> 00:24:23,750
"I don't know which job to choose."

615
00:24:23,800 --> 00:24:25,940
Léa: You can say: "I'm still looking for

616
00:24:25,940 --> 00:24:28,190
the job that suits me."

617
00:24:28,240 --> 00:24:31,190
Mathieu: Or: "I'm still thinking

618
00:24:31,190 --> 00:24:34,300
about my career path."

619
00:24:34,350 --> 00:24:35,890
Léa: Third sentence:

620
00:24:35,940 --> 00:24:38,910
"I want a job that makes me happy."

621
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:41,250
Mathieu: You can say: "I'd like to find

622
00:24:41,250 --> 00:24:44,670
a job where I can flourish."

623
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:46,540
Léa: Or: "I'm looking for a job

624
00:24:46,540 --> 00:24:48,670
that's meaningful to me."

625
00:24:48,720 --> 00:24:52,150
Mathieu: Fourth sentence: "I'm good with people."

626
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:55,710
Léa: You can say: "I have good people skills."

627
00:24:55,760 --> 00:24:57,570
Mathieu: Or: "Human contact

628
00:24:57,570 --> 00:25:00,380
is one of my strengths."

629
00:25:00,430 --> 00:25:01,970
Léa: Fifth sentence:

630
00:25:02,020 --> 00:25:04,590
"I don't have enough experience."

631
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:06,890
Mathieu: You can say: "I still lack

632
00:25:06,890 --> 00:25:09,370
experience in this field,

633
00:25:09,370 --> 00:25:11,830
but I'm ready to learn."

634
00:25:11,880 --> 00:25:15,300
Léa: Or: "I'd like to develop my skills

635
00:25:15,300 --> 00:25:17,070
before applying."

636
00:25:17,120 --> 00:25:20,020
Mathieu: And our last sentence:

637
00:25:20,070 --> 00:25:22,640
"I want to try another job."

638
00:25:22,640 --> 00:25:24,790
Léa: You can say: "I'd like to explore

639
00:25:24,790 --> 00:25:28,140
new career possibilities."

640
00:25:28,190 --> 00:25:31,460
Mathieu: Or: "I'm considering a career change,

641
00:25:31,460 --> 00:25:33,650
but I'd rather find out more

642
00:25:33,650 --> 00:25:35,870
before making a decision."

643
00:25:35,920 --> 00:25:38,540
Léa: Very good. These expressions let us

644
00:25:38,540 --> 00:25:40,000
talk about our plans

645
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:43,160
in a more precise and natural way.

646
00:25:43,160 --> 00:25:45,960
Mathieu: You know, Léa, after this conversation,

647
00:25:45,960 --> 00:25:49,360
I see my childhood dreams differently.

648
00:25:49,360 --> 00:25:51,110
Léa: Oh really? Why?

649
00:25:51,160 --> 00:25:53,960
Mathieu: Because I thought I'd given up

650
00:25:53,960 --> 00:25:55,860
my dream of being an explorer.

651
00:25:55,910 --> 00:25:59,030
But in the end, I still love travelling

652
00:25:59,030 --> 00:26:01,880
and discovering new places.

653
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:04,670
Léa: And your dream of becoming a chef?

654
00:26:04,720 --> 00:26:06,920
Mathieu: It's still there too.

655
00:26:06,920 --> 00:26:09,510
I simply express it by cooking

656
00:26:09,510 --> 00:26:11,720
good meals for my friends.

657
00:26:11,720 --> 00:26:15,240
Léa: There you go! Your dreams haven't disappeared.

658
00:26:15,240 --> 00:26:16,910
They've simply found

659
00:26:16,910 --> 00:26:18,800
another place in your life.

660
00:26:18,800 --> 00:26:21,160
Mathieu: And I think it's a lovely way

661
00:26:21,160 --> 00:26:22,940
to understand our choices.

662
00:26:22,990 --> 00:26:26,200
Léa: Yes. Our dreams can evolve with us.

663
00:26:26,200 --> 00:26:27,540
And sometimes, the job

664
00:26:27,540 --> 00:26:29,600
that suits us today

665
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:30,470
won't be the one

666
00:26:30,470 --> 00:26:32,960
we'll want to do in ten years.

667
00:26:32,960 --> 00:26:35,440
Mathieu: Because we keep learning,

668
00:26:35,440 --> 00:26:37,580
changing and discovering

669
00:26:37,580 --> 00:26:39,800
new possibilities.

670
00:26:39,800 --> 00:26:44,080
Léa: Exactly. So, dear friends, before we finish,

671
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:46,540
we'd like to ask you a question.

672
00:26:46,590 --> 00:26:48,970
Mathieu: What's your dream job?

673
00:26:49,020 --> 00:26:52,050
Or what job would you like to try,

674
00:26:52,050 --> 00:26:54,390
even for just one day?

675
00:26:54,440 --> 00:26:57,280
Léa: In the comments, complete this sentence:

676
00:26:57,280 --> 00:26:59,400
"My dream job would be...,

677
00:26:59,400 --> 00:27:01,640
because..."

678
00:27:01,640 --> 00:27:04,680
Mathieu: And if you're not sure of your choice yet,

679
00:27:04,680 --> 00:27:07,810
you can say: "I'm still

680
00:27:07,810 --> 00:27:10,950
looking for the job that suits me."

681
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:12,960
Léa: One or two sentences are enough.

682
00:27:12,960 --> 00:27:15,140
Don't be afraid of making mistakes.

683
00:27:15,190 --> 00:27:18,320
What matters is practising your French.

684
00:27:18,320 --> 00:27:20,440
Mathieu: And if you enjoyed this episode,

685
00:27:20,440 --> 00:27:23,200
remember to leave a little thumbs-up,

686
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:25,890
subscribe to The Cozy French

687
00:27:25,890 --> 00:27:28,730
and share our conversation

688
00:27:28,730 --> 00:27:31,400
with someone who's learning French.

689
00:27:31,400 --> 00:27:34,360
Léa: Thank you so much for spending this time with us.

690
00:27:34,360 --> 00:27:36,280
We hope this conversation

691
00:27:36,280 --> 00:27:37,760
helped you discover

692
00:27:37,760 --> 00:27:39,400
new expressions

693
00:27:39,400 --> 00:27:41,580
and think about your plans.

694
00:27:41,630 --> 00:27:43,220
Mathieu: Before we go, Léa,

695
00:27:43,220 --> 00:27:46,400
I'd like to ask you one last question.

696
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:49,000
Do you think there's

697
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:52,830
just one ideal job for each person?

698
00:27:52,880 --> 00:27:55,190
Léa: No. I think we can have

699
00:27:55,190 --> 00:27:57,360
several jobs that suit us

700
00:27:57,360 --> 00:28:00,040
at different stages of our life.

701
00:28:00,040 --> 00:28:02,060
Mathieu: So there's no need

702
00:28:02,060 --> 00:28:05,910
to find a perfect profession forever.

703
00:28:05,960 --> 00:28:08,650
Léa: Exactly. Above all, we have to learn

704
00:28:08,650 --> 00:28:09,940
to know ourselves better,

705
00:28:09,990 --> 00:28:12,040
explore the possibilities

706
00:28:12,040 --> 00:28:14,960
and make choices that are meaningful to us.

707
00:28:14,960 --> 00:28:18,350
Mathieu: And if someone hasn't found their path yet?

708
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:20,470
Léa: They can keep learning,

709
00:28:20,470 --> 00:28:21,880
ask questions,

710
00:28:21,880 --> 00:28:23,520
try new things

711
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:25,520
and move forward at their own pace.

712
00:28:25,520 --> 00:28:27,190
Mathieu: What a lovely way

713
00:28:27,190 --> 00:28:29,720
to end our conversation.

714
00:28:29,720 --> 00:28:31,960
Léa: Take care, dear friends,

715
00:28:31,960 --> 00:28:34,720
and remember that your dreams can change.

716
00:28:34,720 --> 00:28:36,790
It's not necessarily a failure,

717
00:28:36,790 --> 00:28:39,060
it's also part of life.

718
00:28:39,110 --> 00:28:40,210
Mathieu: This was Mathieu...

719
00:28:40,210 --> 00:28:42,960
Léa: And Léa, from The Cozy French!

720
00:28:42,960 --> 00:28:45,040
Mathieu: See you very soon

721
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:47,960
for a new conversation in French.

722
00:28:47,960 --> 00:28:49,750
Léa: Keep learning,

723
00:28:49,750 --> 00:28:53,080
discovering and believing in your possibilities.

724
00:28:53,080 --> 00:28:54,350
See you very soon!

725
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:56,110
Mathieu: See you soon, dear friends!

726
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:57,880
Léa: See you soon!
